What was vendored
-----------------
ALL published examples (passed / failed / inapplicable) of all three image rules,
so the set is the complete rule set and not a favorable subset — the same rule
act-gold/NOTICE states. Which cases the pipeline can actually reach is then a
MEASURED property, recorded in `image_reachability.json`, not a choice made while
fetching:

  Image accessible name is descriptive                      (ruleId qt1vmo)  16 cases
  Image not in the accessibility tree is decorative         (ruleId e88epe)  20 cases
  DEPRECATED — Image filename is accessible name for image  (ruleId 9eb3f6)  15 cases

  html/    : the 51 referenced HTML files for the three rules above.
  assets/  : the 10 image files those cases reference, stored at their UPSTREAM
             path, so a render can request them exactly as ACT wrote them.

One referenced asset is deliberately absent upstream
----------------------------------------------------
`/test-assets/does-not-exist.png` returns HTTP 404 and is referenced by two cases
(`96471b44c9…` qt1vmo and `99e3d03366…` e88epe), both of which ACT marks
`inapplicable`. This is not a fetch failure: "the image request did not complete"
is a state these rules are explicitly written about, and their applicability
excludes it. It is recorded in `assets.json` with its status and left out of
`assets/`, so a render of those cases fails exactly as it does upstream.

What actually made these cases render blank
-------------------------------------------
The ACT case HTML references its assets at the site-absolute path `/test-assets/…`,
which under a `file://` render resolves to the filesystem root and loads nothing.
The vendored bytes are NOT rewritten to fix that — the ACT markup is expert
material and is kept byte-exact. The scanner repairs it at the request layer
instead (`clearway/scanner/scan.py`, `_vendored_asset_route`), and every pool case
then renders with `naturalWidth > 0`.

Why the served Content-Type is recorded beside the upstream one
---------------------------------------------------------------
Five of these assets are extensionless and are served `application/octet-stream`
upstream. That was expected to be a second cause of blank renders; it is NOT —
measured against the pinned Chromium, an `<img>` decodes from its bytes under
`application/octet-stream`, under `text/plain`, and with no Content-Type at all.
The honest type is still recorded and served, for two reasons that are real: an
image handed to a multimodal model travels as `data:<media-type>;base64,…` and is
decoded by that declared type, which neither the extensionless file name nor the
upstream header can supply; and a truthful response is what keeps this correct if
anything ever sends `X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff`. `assets.json` records both
types per asset.

